{"requirements":[{"id":"filesystem-quota","requirement":"On node1, limit datauser to 100 MB of storage under /srv/data using filesystem-level enforcement.","status":"satisfied","summary":"datauser exists with /srv/data as its home, and the directory is owned by datauser with mode 0700. XFS user-quota accounting and enforcement are enabled, with 100M soft and hard limits. Direct write tests before and after restart were stopped with “Disk quota exceeded,” and the quota, account, mount, and existing data recovered after a confirmed boot-ID change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b5f26c04c5e4fa4","cmd-845eebdbcbab41f2","cmd-c7d4927a59054e62","cmd-70ef1551e6f948d2","cmd-795912ab47554720","cmd-45b6cdb2b64d4963","cmd-acbdb7c0a3104cfa","cmd-2200d832f28b45d9"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"Required mutations were well scoped: creating the account and directory, enabling persistent XFS quotas, setting the limit, rebooting to activate and verify persistence, and performing bounded enforcement tests. Test files were removed, package health remained sound, no failed services appeared, and network exposure was unchanged. Two minor artifacts remained: /srv/data/quota-persistence-proof.txt from the restart test, explicitly visible in the final state, and an /etc/fstab.before-datauser-quota backup created without captured cleanup. Both are low-risk but unnecessary retained residue. No other unnecessary mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c8b57317928f4a16","cmd-70ef1551e6f948d2","cmd-2200d832f28b45d9","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The requested filesystem-enforced 100 MB limit is satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: it records a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, recovered account/data/quota state, and successful post-restart enforcement. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The XFS user quota is attached to the root filesystem, so it limits all data owned by datauser on that filesystem rather than accounting exclusively within /srv/data. This still directly enforces the requested maximum under /srv/data but is broader in scope.","The final contents of /etc were not inventoried; retention of /etc/fstab.before-datauser-quota is inferred from its successful creation and the absence of a cleanup command in the complete timeline."]}
